LOGBOOK.md:1707:- EVIDENCE: `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck -section 15.2` exited **0**. §15.2 is the nineteen-row normative exit-code registry (SPEC.md:11073-11095) and nothing in the tree implements it — the only `os.Exit` calls are the `exit(1)` paths of `cataloggen` and `tracecheck`. Same for `-section 7.3`, the closed Provider Manifest, whose sole trace is a catalog row naming its URN. Both were bound to `internal/catalog/catalog.go:ForRelease`, an unrelated symbol.
LOGBOOK.md-1708-- MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED: the blind spot is **19 of the 157** pinned headings (7.3, 10.8.1, 13.5, 13.12, 13.14.1-13.14.5, 14.3, 14.6, 15.2, 16.6, 16.7, 18.2, 19.4, Appendices A/B/C). `TestUnmeasuredCoverageIsAScannerBlindSpotNotAnAbsenceOfObligation` re-derives that set from the pinned document and shows every one of them has a substantive body (8-272 non-blank lines). Not one is a heading with nothing to discharge, so the "declarative" reading was false for all 19, not just the two that were bound.
LOGBOOK.md-1709-- AGGRAVATOR: `verifySectionBindingCoverage` *refused* a gap on the `declarative` bucket, so §15.2 was structurally forbidden from disclosing that nothing implements exit codes. The disclosure mechanism the change built was unavailable to exactly the bindings that needed it most.
LOGBOOK.md-1710-- DECISION: the bucket is renamed `unmeasured`, it now owes the same mandatory gap as every level below `full`, and it is **dropped from the admitted set entirely** — admission requires `full` and nothing else. The alternative was to admit on a reviewed justification sentence, and that was rejected: the gate cannot verify such a sentence, so unlocking admission with one is self-minted evidence for precisely the class this gate exists to refuse. Absence of a scanner-visible clause is a failure to read, and a fallback defined for absence must not fire on a failure to read.
LOGBOOK.md-1711-- NOT RE-BOUND: §7.3, §13.14.5 and §15.2 keep their bindings and carry honest gaps. `unowned_sections` was not an option — `expectedCatalogSectionBindings` requires an owner for all three — and re-binding them to a friendlier symbol is the failure this board has hit three times. §13.14.5's `validateSessionEventV2` is at least topically the section's subject and its gap says so; §7.3's and §15.2's say the implementation does not exist.
