LOGBOOK.md-1799-### 2237 — A fault suite that only proves the call failed proves the cheap half
LOGBOOK.md-1800-
LOGBOOK.md-1801-- FINDING (review round 2, blocking): seven fault boundaries were pinned at 2204, but only three carried a recovery half. Staged mode drift, both FIFO shard cases and BOTH quarantine-ENOSPC subtests asserted the failure state and stopped. Anything can fail; what a durability suite exists to establish is what a later run can still do. Both quarantine-ENOSPC cases are full-disk cases, so this was a direct miss against the front-loaded rule that full-disk and short-write cases must show the store is left recoverable.
LOGBOOK.md-1802-- FINDING: `README.md` then described all seven as sharing four properties — digest path absent, no staged residue, nothing manufactured into quarantine, retry installs the declared bytes. Four of the seven did not carry all four, and for the existing-quarantine-denied case two of the four are FALSE BY DESIGN and the tests correctly assert the opposite: the corrupt object stays at the digest path and the candidate IS preserved in quarantine. The very next sentence stated that correct behavior, so the section contradicted itself across two adjacent sentences. This is the capability-claim-that-does-not-reproduce shape; a later contributor would read recovery-after-fault as pinned at boundaries where nothing reddens if it breaks.
