LOGBOOK.md-1547-- FINDING (reviewer Observation B, acted on): retry independence from the exit status was proven at exit 12 alone, and the one exit-15 envelope any test drove already declared `retryable: true`, so a fabricated "if the status is 15, retry" branch returned the answer the document would have given and survived. `TestTheRetryDecisionFollowsTheDocumentAtEveryFailureStatus` drives both polarities across the whole failure domain; the true arm covers fourteen of seventeen statuses because the pinned document forbids the claim for every code of the other three, and that figure is pinned rather than left implicit.
LOGBOOK.md-1548-- DECISION (reviewer Observation C, left alone): the two survivors the round-3 reviewer checked as genuinely equivalent — `Succeeded()` recomputed from `exitStatus`, and `ExitStatus()` returned from `failure.ExitCode()` — are NOT "fixed". They survive because `readSuccess` and `readFailure` between them make document and status provably agree on every admitted reading. A survivor dismissed with a reason is evidence; one quietly fixed hides why it survived. The same holds for the two round-2 dismissals, the near-equivalent `details` narrowing of `requireCommonDataModel` and the `CodesByExitStatus` empty-group pre-seed.
