LOGBOOK.md:1942:- FINDING: `-ivan@peer.example` is the shape that proves the leading-hyphen clause is load-bearing rather than redundant with the grammar. The `[user@]host[:port]` rules alone admit it, because `-` is a legal login-name byte, so only the leading-hyphen clause refuses it.
LOGBOOK.md-1943-- REGRESSION: review found that claim overclaimed as shipped. None of the eight cases in `TestLoadRefusesOptionLikeMeshEndpoints` carries an `@`, so every one of them is *also* refused by the host clause once the hyphen clause is gone; they pinned the clause's reason and its ordering, never its coverage class, and the `-ivan@peer.example` proof lived only in a `.temp` probe that never reached the committed tests. A single-clause narrowing to `strings.HasPrefix(endpoint, "-") && !strings.Contains(endpoint, "@")` therefore kept the whole `internal/config` suite green while admitting `-ivan@peer.example`, which `ssh(1)` parses as `-i` with identity file `van@peer.example` — a live injection.
