LOGBOOK.md-1945-- DECISION: the isolation rule is now structural for the whole endpoint grammar, not applied only to the two shapes review named. Every refusal case in `endpointGrammarCases` carries a `neighbour` — the same endpoint with only the named violation removed — that must be admitted, so a case cannot come to rest on a clause other than the one it names; `TestEveryRefusedMeshEndpointCaseDeclaresAnIsolatingNeighbour` reddens if a later case is added without one.
LOGBOOK.md-1946-- FINDING: the whitespace clause is the one clause that cannot be pinned by admission — no whitespace-carrying endpoint is spellable by a login name or a DNS label, so the grammar refuses the same inputs with a different reason. It is pinned by its named reason and its ordering ahead of the grammar instead, and the entry says so rather than claiming an admission gap it does not close.
LOGBOOK.md-1947-- DECISION: `endpoint = "peer.example:022"` is admitted; a leading-zero port reads as decimal 22 and carries no option or whitespace. Review flagged that the case named `padded port` pinned only the 1..5-byte length bound, so the name overclaimed. `:022` is now pinned as admitted reviewed behaviour, and the length bound gets cases that rest on it alone (`:000022` and `:0000022`, decimal 22 and inside the numeric bound, so only `len(port) > 5` refuses them).
LOGBOOK.md-1948-- FINDING: bare-IPv6-with-port was unpinned. Widening `admitEndpointHost` to accept anything `net.ParseIP` accepts survived the old suite, because the only bare-IPv6 case carried no port and never reached the widened branch; `2001:db8::1:2222` and `::1:22` now close it (M17).
LOGBOOK.md-1949-- EVIDENCE: 28 single-clause mutants over `endpoint.go`, `sshargs.go` and the `validateMesh` wiring, 26 killed and 2 proven equivalent. Each killed mutant reddens only its own cases: M01-M04 and M24 (leading-hyphen deleted, narrowed to `-o`, narrowed to `--`, carved out for `@`, and reordered behind whitespace) redden the option-shaped tests; M17 (host widened to anything `net.ParseIP` accepts) reddens the two bare-IPv6-with-port cases; M18 (port length upper bound dropped) reddens `port over length bound` alone; M26/M27 (`sshPermittedFlags` gains `-A`, `sshPermittedValueFlags` gains `-L`) redden the key-set pin and the named capability-widening refusals. Mutants that delete a clause and leave its import unused are reported as INVALID-BUILD rather than counted as kills. Deleting `host == ""` leaves `strings.Split("", ".")` returning one empty label that `admitEndpointHostLabel` refuses with the same reason; dropping `len(port) < 1` leaves the empty port at `number == 0`, which `number >= 1` refuses with the same reason. Both were confirmed equivalent by a differential dump of `admitMeshEndpoint` over a generated corpus, byte-identical to baseline. Harness and logs: `.temp/BUG-260902-4ajzyz/mutants2.py`, `mutants-04.log`, `equiv-*.txt`. That set was *not* exhaustive — see the boundary-adjacency regression below.
LOGBOOK.md-1950-- REGRESSION: rev2 review found one live surviving mutant, and it was introduced by the previous cycle: narrowing `admitEndpointPort` from `len(port) > 5` to `> 6` kept the whole suite green while admitting `peer.example:000022`. The case that owned the clause, `port over length bound`, uses a *seven*-byte port, which stays refused under the narrowed bound — so it pinned the bound to somewhere in [5,6] rather than to 5. The reviewed baseline patch of BUG-260902-3c7ovg had a six-byte `padded port` case that killed this mutant exactly, and rev2 replaced it. The earlier claim that both surviving mutants were equivalent was true of the 28 mutants that cycle ran, but that set contained only the *deletion* of this bound, never its one-step narrowing, and the entry above is corrected to say so. No exposure: the strings newly admitted are six-byte zero-padded ports whose decimal value is already inside 1..65535 and which `ssh(1)` resolves identically. The defect was in the evidence, not the gate.
