LOGBOOK.md-1907-- FINDING: `core_records.go` validated Session Event `predecessors` with `validateSortedUniqueDigests`, so a duplicate predecessor was **refused**. Pinned Section 5.2 (SPEC.md:1728) declares "`predecessors` as a **sorted** array of one or more record/event digests". Section 1.6 (SPEC.md:323) defines `sorted unique T[n..m]` as the COMPOUND phrase meaning "bytewise canonical ordering **and no duplicate**", and the document applies that phrase systematically — `event_heads`, `manifest_ids`, `evidence_ids`, `object_ids`, `accepted_risks`, `sanitized_remote_urls`, `agent_project_config_paths` — while using bare `sorted` for `predecessors` alone. Reading the two as synonyms invented a refusal. This was the FIFTH invented constraint on this leaf; the previous four were each fixed one at a time.
LOGBOOK.md:1908:- FINDING, found by the new gate rather than by review: the SAME error sat in `validateWorkspaceGroupRecord`. Section 2 (SPEC.md:2146) declares "Members are sorted by `workspace_id`, and no two members may have an equal or case-colliding `group_relative_path`" — the declared uniqueness is on `group_relative_path`, never on `workspace_id`. Production refused two members sharing a `workspace_id`. Repaired, and the admission is now pinned by an accepting case at both identity entries.
LOGBOOK.md-1909-- ROOT CAUSE, one level up from "read the spec more carefully": the document uses a CONTROLLED VOCABULARY for array constraints and the implementation treated one of its phrases as a synonym of another. Judgement is the failure mode, so judgement had to leave the loop.
LOGBOOK.md-1910-- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/array_order_inventory_test.go` plus `testdata/array-order-constraints.md`. Ordering sites are derived structurally — a comparison between a value derived from the ranged element and a value bound to an EARLIER element, either carried forward or indexed at an offset from the loop key — so no rename hides one. Strength is read off the comparison OPERATOR (`<=` refuses duplicates, `<` admits them), and the member name is derived by tracing the ranged collection back to the string literal that named it, through helper parameters and across call sites. Order and uniqueness are pinned as SEPARATE factors with their own SPEC lines, because the document frequently declares them at different lines; `sorted unique` must cite a uniqueness declaration and bare `sorted` must cite none. A strengthened validator then cannot be written down consistently.

